/-! ## Goldbach representations on the `K = 2` wheel

The `K = 2` wheel is the wheel generated by the first two primes, so its modulus is
`2 * 3 = 6` and its spokes are the residues `1` and `5` modulo `6`.

`GoldbachWheelK2 m` says that every even number `n` with `10 ≤ n ≤ m` is a sum of two
primes **both of which lie on the wheel**, i.e. both are coprime to `6` (equivalently,
both are at least `5`). The lower bound `10` is sharp for this strengthened form: `6`
and `8` are sums of two primes only by using the off-wheel prime `3`.

The family is indexed by the bound `m`; this file adds the new member `m = 1051`.
-/

/-- The modulus of the `K = 2` wheel: the product of the first two primes. -/

private theorem coprime_wheelK2 {p : ℕ} (hp : Nat.Prime p) (h5 : 5 ≤ p) :
    Nat.Coprime p wheelK2Modulus := by
  rw [Nat.Prime.coprime_iff_not_dvd hp]
  intro hdvd
  have h6 : p ≤ 6 := by
    simpa [wheelK2Modulus] using Nat.le_of_dvd (by norm_num [wheelK2Modulus]) hdvd
  revert hdvd
  interval_cases p
  · decide
  · exact absurd hp (by decide)

/-- Candidate small summands: the wheel primes below `80`. -/
